+### Selected release headset bootstrap
+
+`experiment_headset_setup_enabled` is a strict JSON Boolean, default false.
+The selected app must also select the experiment command gateway and original
+panel, and declare exactly `ExperimentSessionHeadsetSetupProvider` in its provider
+surface. The resolver binds the two `src/headset-setup` source hashes into the
+resolution/feature lock and emits the package-scoped `.headset-setup` authority
+with DUMP permission. The builder checks that exact source/provider closure.
+The general debug provider and its classes remain absent from this release lane.
+
+This is attended shell provisioning for device qualification, not end-user phone
+onboarding or a new BLE/LAN bootstrap route. The provider independently checks
+calling UID 2000, DUMP and selected manifest metadata before decoding. Its sole
+method is `headset-setup`, with the same bounded base64 JSON encoding as the
+debug CLI, but only `status`, `provision-headset-identity` and `enroll-trusted-lan`
+are accepted. Status and receipts retain the existing `operator_command.v1` /
+`operator_receipt.v1` schemas. No experiment or private-sensor command is exposed.
+
+Use `Invoke-ExperimentSessionControl.ps1 -ReleaseHeadsetSetup` with the normal
+exact ADB hash, transport, canonical serial, boot ID, package and request path.
+The CLI first requires the selected process already running; it does not launch
+one through the provider. A status request contains only `schema` and `operation`.
+Its receipt contains PID, UID, process start ticks, installed APK SHA256,
+`snapshot` (selected activation binding), `native_response.readback` (native
+session fences), `headset_setup` (non-secret current identity/network/build and
+discovery metadata), and `local_enrollment` (native-owner/epoch/lifecycle offer).
+
+Both mutations require all existing actor/APK/binding/session fields, a fresh
+16-hex `operation_id`, `operator_origin: "shell-headset-setup-v1"`, positive
+`expected_native_owner_identity` and `expected_gateway_lifecycle_revision`, plus
+the latest 32-hex `expected_enrollment_reference`. Identity provisioning adds
+only `hardware_serial`; the CLI requires it equal live `ro.serialno` and the
+selected canonical serial. Trusted-LAN enrollment instead adds only the exact
+Boolean `trusted_lan_consent: true`. Extra, missing and mistyped fields fail.
+Neither operation bypasses the gateway's current idle/native-owner guards.
+
+Every privileged status replaces the offer. Both mutations consume their offer
+and operation ID before any effect; the offer expires after five seconds. A lost
+response remains unknown and cannot authorize replay. Actual/stored serial
+conflicts remain rejected; same-value provisioning is idempotent. A newly stored
+serial advances the gateway lifecycle and restarts discovery from that identity,
+so read fresh status before enrollment. Enrollment uses the existing shared
+rotation and guarded persistence route, with credentials written only to the
+CLI's create-new protected `enrollment.json`, never normal output.
+
+`experiment_panel_initially_closed` is a separate strict Boolean, default false.
+It selects the compile-bound native generation initializer through
+`RUSTY_QUEST_NATIVE_RENDERER_PANEL_INITIALLY_CLOSED`. The first native frame starts
+closed and remains pending until the existing renderer acknowledges it. Existing
+products retain the visible default. Triple-B reopening, actual first-use Android
+permission requests/retries and `open_control_default` keep their current owners.
+
+Focused checks: `Test-ExperimentSessionHeadsetSetupSelection.ps1`,
+`test_experiment_headset_setup.py`, `test_experiment_headset_setup_cli.py`,
+`Test-ExperimentSessionHeadsetSetupAndroid.ps1` (current changed Java against real
+selected shell classes and Android API), and the native
+`selected_initial_panel_is_closed_before_first_frame_and_can_reopen` unit.
+These host checks do not establish device permissions, BLE advertising, a phone
+connection or visible headset presentation. The broader historical
+`Test-NativeRendererExperimentPanel.ps1` currently has stale pre-javac literal
+assertions for the earlier Activity facade; record that separately from focused
+current-source compiler evidence.
+
+### Selected phone pairing and saved-phone UI
+
+`experiment_phone_pairing_enabled` is a strict default-off Boolean. Selection
+requires the existing experiment gateway and original panel, emits
+`rusty.quest.experiment.phone_pairing.enabled: true`, and locks the exact current
+pairing protocol, trusted-phone registry, Android UI bridge, pure UI policy and
+selected startup-permission coordinator (five exact sources)
+source hashes in `phone_pairing_sources`. The protocol/registry definitions also
+belong to the existing explicit panel source closure because the gateway uses
+their types. Their new behavior stays inactive when metadata is absent. The two
+wearer UI definitions and startup coordinator compile only in the selected phone-pairing source set.
+Selection does not enable the separate shell/DUMP provisioning provider.
+
+Selected Remote discovery and HTTP identity use v2 installation identity. The
+exact discovery fields are `v`, `identity_kind`, `device`, `serial`, `epoch`,
+`native_epoch`, `mode`, `gateways` and `pairing`. `device` is a stable application
+installation ID; `serial` is the actual hardware serial when available and empty
+otherwise. V1 keeps its nonempty hardware-serial contract. Discovery grants no
+commands or private data. Nearby new phones use the proof-bound `pair-*` exchange;
+only both actual device confirmations permit atomic membership persistence.
+
+The registry retains up to eight independent members/keys, with atomic migration
+of a valid legacy enrollment. Explicit machine provisioning may add one member
+without rotating unrelated phones. Selected unaffiliated guests remain read-only.
+Authenticated `controller_access` status contains `v`, `role`, `active_phone` and
+`revision`. Claim/release uses action `controller-access` and the closed payload
+`{v:1,id:<16-hex>,op:"claim"|"release",expected_control_revision:<integer>}`.
+Control begins unowned; a live controller is never stolen. Only that controller's
+authenticated status renews its 15-second lease. Revocation invalidates cached
+protocols. A handover cancels old deferred main-start/recenter work without
+finalizing or replaying a recording.
+
+The headset UI polls its current main gateway owner while the actual Activity
+is resumed, even when the native panel starts closed. Startup and ordinary idle
+polling never open phone approval. Only a real incoming request whose ordered
+commitment opening and proof have been verified becomes a pending prompt; the
+initial `pair-begin` alone presents no code or approval. That pending request presents
+the exact frozen six-digit code and bounded phone label with Allow and Reject
+buttons. It uses the existing native dialog/window/input path and never creates
+a keyboard or exposes a pairing decision through the typed product operator.
+The gateway rechecks its idle/calibration, request, membership and owner guards;
+the UI never approves from a readback, timer, visibility event or timeout.
+
+Each real button callback rechecks the captured Activity/controller, native
+producer, pairing ID, displayed code/label and current expiration. A retired,
+cancelled or replaced request cannot inherit an old callback. Dismissal restores
+panel visibility only when that exact producer and opening request revision
+still belong to the prompt. A manual panel reopen/close or another producer is
+preserved. Pause, destruction and request expiration dismiss the old dialog.
+Existing first-use Android permission requests and triple-B reopening remain
+independent; pairing waits while the actual host lacks window focus.
+The selected actual native host coordinates Polar, legacy carrier and discovery
+runtime permissions in one ordinary Android request after resume, including with
+the app panel closed. It requests only missing declared SCAN/CONNECT/ADVERTISE,
+fine/coarse location and Nearby Wi-Fi permissions appropriate to the device API.
+Permission intent is independent of trusted experiment cold-launch admission;
+session/sensor effects keep their existing owners and gates. Completion is posted
+back to the three owners after pending state is cleared. An outstanding request
+survives pause/resume; denial does not loop and an empty cancellation permits only
+one later-resume retry. Other product selections retain their existing routes.
+Bluetooth-power approval follows the settled runtime request. This does not select the Wi-Fi setup GATT
+characteristic or give public legacy BLE experiment commands new authority.
+
+App permission grants do not establish global Location-services readiness. If a
+selected product declares Polar SCAN, its runtime permissions are granted and
+Location services are actually disabled, startup opens the ordinary Android
+Location-settings surface once for that exact resumed host under the kiosk
+system-prompt fence. Only the wearer can change the setting. Return rechecks the
+existing Polar owner; leaving Location off does not repeat the settings surface.
+No app/pairing panel is opened and no setting is written programmatically.
+Compact Polar setup adds optional `y.l`, separate from `p` and `b`, with values
+`enabled`, `disabled`, `unavailable`, `legacy-unobserved`, `permission-blocked` or
+`unknown`. A disabled scan prerequisite does not invalidate connected streams.
+
+This uses the native Android permission/result flow described in
+[Android Bluetooth permissions](https://developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/bluetooth/bt-permissions)
+and [Meta's native Android Location guidance](https://developers.meta.com/vr/documentation/native/android/move-location/).
+[Android's Activity implementation](https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/refs/heads/master/core/java/android/app/Activity.java)
+cancels a second simultaneous runtime request with empty result arrays; independent
+Polar and discovery requests therefore cannot stand in for a coordinated closure.
+With phone pairing selected, the legacy public BLE write, prepare/execute and
+queued main-effect paths reject mutations even with an open-control default or
+valid legacy code. Read-only nonprivate status/receipts and the internal gateway
+journal retain their existing routes; metadata-off legacy behavior is unchanged.
+
+Selected products show bounded saved-phone labels, an active-controller label,
+individual Remove buttons and Release phone control. Removal requires an actual
+confirmation button; release checks the current control revision before calling
+the registry. No membership keys are displayed. The selected surface replaces
+the legacy manual key enrollment and open-BLE-control choices. New phones pair
+through the gateway `pair-*` exchange, with independent confirmations on phone
+and headset, rather than laptop/manual credential transfer. Public discovery is
+read-only; installation identity and hardware serial keep separate meanings.
+
+Focused checks are `Test-ExperimentPhonePairingSelection.ps1`,
+`Test-ExperimentPhonePairingUi.ps1` (pure ownership policy and actual UI bridge
+with inert Android boundaries), and `Test-ExperimentPhonePairingAndroid.ps1`
+(all current changed Java against actual selected shell classes and Android API).
+Protocol/registry checks include `Test-ExperimentPhonePairing.ps1`,
+`test_experiment_trusted_phones.py` and the existing gateway host harness.
+Startup checks include `test_experiment_startup_permissions.py` (actual coordinator,
+reflective adapter, completion mapping and NativeActivity lifecycle; compiled
+missing-SCAN/overlap negative controls) and `test_polar_platform_projection.py`.
+Keep these software/compiler checks separate from real headset visibility,
+first-use permissions, phone discovery, pairing and calibration acceptance.
+
